Represents a generic type argument attribute. It allows you to create custom generic type argument such as , , etc.


[GenericTypeArgument]
internal interface TTMy: IMy { }

Specifies injection order priority for constructors, methods, properties, and fields.
While this attribute is part of the DI API, you can implement custom ordering attributes in any namespace.

For constructors, it defines the order in which constructors are considered:

class Service: IService
{
    private readonly string _name;

    [Ordinal(1)]
    public Service(IDependency dependency) =>
        _name = "with dependency";

    [Ordinal(0)]
    public Service(string name) => _name = name;
}


For fields, properties, and methods, it defines the order of injection:

class Person: IPerson
{
    private readonly string _name = "";

    [Ordinal(0)]
    public int Id;

    [Ordinal(1)]
    public string FirstName
    {
        set
        {
            _name = value;
        }
    }

    public IDependency? Dependency { get; private set; }

    [Ordinal(2)]
    public void SetDependency(IDependency dependency) =>
        Dependency = dependency;
}

Represents a tag attribute that overrides an injection tag. The tag can be a constant, a type, or an enum value.
This attribute is part of the API, but you can use your own attribute and define it in any assembly or namespace.

Sometimes it's important to take control of building a dependency graph. For example, when there are multiple implementations of the same contract. In this case, tags will help:

interface IDependency { }


class AbcDependency: IDependency { }


class XyzDependency: IDependency { }


class Dependency: IDependency { }


interface IService
{
    IDependency Dependency1 { get; }


    IDependency Dependency2 { get; }
}


class Service: IService
{
    public Service(
        [Tag("Abc")] IDependency dependency1,
        [Tag("Xyz")] IDependency dependency2)
    {
        Dependency1 = dependency1;
        Dependency2 = dependency2;
    }

    public IDependency Dependency1 { get; }


    public IDependency Dependency2 { get; }
}


DI.Setup("Composition")
    .Bind<IDependency>("Abc").To<AbcDependency>()
    .Bind<IDependency>("Xyz").To<XyzDependency>()
    .Bind<IService>().To<Service>().Root<IService>("Root");

The injection type can be defined manually using the Type attribute. This attribute explicitly overrides an injected type; otherwise it is determined automatically based on the type of the constructor/method parameter, property, or field.
This attribute is part of the API, but you can use your own attribute and define it in any assembly or namespace.


interface IDependency { }


class AbcDependency: IDependency { }


class XyzDependency: IDependency { }


interface IService
{
    IDependency Dependency1 { get; }

    IDependency Dependency2 { get; }
}


class Service: IService
{
    public Service(
        [Type(typeof(AbcDependency))] IDependency dependency1,
        [Type(typeof(XyzDependency))] IDependency dependency2)
    {
        Dependency1 = dependency1;
        Dependency2 = dependency2;
    }


    public IDependency Dependency1 { get; }


    public IDependency Dependency2 { get; }
}


DI.Setup("Composition")
    .Bind<IService>().To<Service>().Root<IService>("Root");

Represents a lifetime attribute that overrides an implementation binding lifetime. This attribute is part of the API, but you can use your own attribute and define it in any assembly or namespace.
Applying the attribute to the implementation makes every binding to it use the specified lifetime:
[Lifetime(Lifetime.Singleton)] class Dependency: IDependency { }
DI.Setup("Composition") .Bind<IDependency>().To<Dependency>() .Root<IDependency>("Root");

Indicates that an implementation type can be automatically added as a binding, optionally specifying the contract type, lifetime, and tags. This lets an implementation declare its own binding instead of listing it explicitly in the setup.
Bind an implementation to a specific contract as a singleton with a tag:
[Bind(typeof(IDependency), Lifetime.Singleton, "my tag")] class Dependency: IDependency { }

Indicates that a property or method can be automatically exported as a binding.


internal class DependencyProvider
{
    [Export()]
    public Dependency Dep => new Dependency();
}


internal class DependencyProvider
{
    [Export(typeof(IDependency<TT>), Lifetime.Singleton)]
    public Dependency GetDep<T>() => new Dependency();
}


internal class DependencyProvider
{
    [Export(typeof(IDependency), Lifetime.PerResolve, "some tag")]
    public Dependency GetDep(int id) => new Dependency(id);
}

